# Build configuration for a fuzzing hostapd. Copy to hostapd/.config:
#
#   cp defconfig-fuzz .config && make
#
# This is defconfig plus the options the WPA3 fuzzing target needs. Building
# with ASan is strongly recommended -- the whole point is to catch memory
# errors, and without it you only see the ones that happen to segfault:
#
#   make clean
#   make CFLAGS="-fsanitize=address -fno-omit-frame-pointer -g -O1" \
#        LDFLAGS="-fsanitize=address"
#
# Regenerated 2026-11-18 against upstream defconfig at 320eb1e09a. Every option
# below was checked to still exist there.

# The fuzzing hooks themselves. Without this the mutation call sites in
# src/ap compile away to nothing and hostapd behaves as a normal AP.
CONFIG_FUZZ=y

CONFIG_DRIVER_NL80211=y
CONFIG_LIBNL32=y

CONFIG_IPV6=y
CONFIG_IEEE80211AX=y
CONFIG_IEEE80211AC=y
CONFIG_ACS=y

# Not optional on this upstream: hostap 320eb1e09a does not build without
# AC + AX + BE together. he_oper_chwidth and eht_oper_chwidth are declared
# under CONFIG_IEEE80211AX / CONFIG_IEEE80211BE in ap_config.h but used
# unguarded in ctrl_iface_ap.c and ctrl_iface.c, and without AC the link
# fails because ieee80211_ml_build_assoc_resp() calls hostapd_eid_vht_*().
# Drop these once upstream fixes the guards.
CONFIG_IEEE80211BE=y

# WPA3-Personal. CONFIG_SAE is the one that matters here; without it the SAE
# authentication path this target exercises is compiled out entirely.
CONFIG_SAE=y

# hostap's own fault-injection knobs. Worth having available alongside the
# fuzzing hooks -- ext_mgmt_frame_handling, ext_eapol_frame_io and
# sae_commit_override can drive frames from outside without patching C.
CONFIG_TESTING_OPTIONS=y

CONFIG_RSN_PREAUTH=y
CONFIG_EAP=y
CONFIG_ERP=y
CONFIG_EAP_MD5=y
CONFIG_EAP_TLS=y
CONFIG_EAP_MSCHAPV2=y
CONFIG_EAP_PEAP=y
CONFIG_EAP_TTLS=y
CONFIG_PKCS12=y
CONFIG_INTERNAL_LIBTOMMATH=y

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Added upstream since this harness was written. All are off here, matching
# upstream defconfig, but the first three add new element construction to the
# Authentication and Association Response frames this harness already mutates,
# so turning them on widens the transmit surface rather than just adding
# features. They are upstream-experimental: expect to be the first to fuzz
# them, and expect findings there to be less interesting than findings in
# shipped code.
#
#CONFIG_IEEE8021X_AUTH=y   # 802.1X (EAP) in Authentication frames, P802.11bi
#CONFIG_PQC=y              # post-quantum key exchange in Authentication frames
#CONFIG_ENC_ASSOC=y        # encrypted association frames (needed by the above)
#CONFIG_PMKSA_PRIVACY=y
#CONFIG_ROBUST_AV=y
#CONFIG_AFC=y
#
# CONFIG_OWE is NOT at the upstream default: upstream defconfig sets it and
# this file does not. See the accounting block at the end.

# WNM on the AP side: WNM-Sleep Mode Response, BSS Transition Management
# Request and Collocated Interference Request are frames hostapd builds and
# transmits, and src/ap/wnm_ap.c carries a mutation hook for each. Without
# this the hooks compile away and those six transmit paths are unreachable --
# not under-fuzzed, absent. CONFIG_WNM defines CONFIG_WNM_AP as well.
#
# The WNM-Sleep Mode Response is the one worth having: it carries GTK, IGTK
# and BIGTK subelements whose lengths the receiver parses, which is where the
# recent upstream subelement length fixes landed.
CONFIG_WNM=y
# Operating Channel Validation. The OCI KDE goes into the EAPOL-Key key data
# and the OCI element into the WNM-Sleep Mode Response -- both frames this
# tree hooks -- so with the WNM hooks in place this adds content to frames
# already under mutation rather than frames nothing touches.
CONFIG_OCV=y


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE THIS BUILD SITS RELATIVE TO UPSTREAM defconfig
#
# Every setting on which this file differs from hostapd/defconfig is
# accounted for below, and fuzzing/config/config.py --check fails both on a
# delta with no line and on a line whose delta has gone away. That is not
# bookkeeping. Turning a flag on to reach code is a decision about what a
# finding MEANS -- a defect behind a flag that ships commented out is a
# weaker claim than one in a default build -- and a run has to be able to say
# which decision it made. The ledger prints the answer beside the numbers.
#
# One line may cover several options where it is one decision.

# WHY CONFIG_FUZZ: the hooks. Everything else here is downstream of wanting
#   them: without it every mutation call site in src/ap compiles away.
# WHY CONFIG_IEEE80211AC CONFIG_IEEE80211AX CONFIG_IEEE80211BE: not a choice.
#   Upstream does not build with its own defconfig at this pin -- the detail
#   is in the comment above CONFIG_IEEE80211BE. Forced here, and the three
#   come out together once upstream guards those three uses.
# WHY CONFIG_SAE: WPA3-Personal is the target this build exists for. Without
#   it the SAE authentication path is compiled out and four transmit hooks
#   have no call site.
# WHY CONFIG_OCV: adds the OCI KDE to the EAPOL-Key key data and the OCI
#   element to the WNM-Sleep Mode Response -- content inside frames already
#   under mutation, rather than new frames.
# WHY CONFIG_WNM: six transmit hooks in src/ap/wnm_ap.c have no call site
#   without it. Absent, not under-fuzzed.
# WHY CONFIG_TESTING_OPTIONS: ext_mgmt_frame_handling and ext_eapol_frame_io
#   drive frames from outside without patching C. It is NOT free -- it also
#   changes real behaviour on the DPP and PASN paths -- so a finding from
#   this build carries it as a condition.
# WHY CONFIG_INTERNAL_LIBTOMMATH: INERT, and left in place with this said
#   out loud. Its -DCONFIG_INTERNAL_LIBTOMMATH sits inside
#   `ifeq ($(CONFIG_CRYPTO), internal)` in the Makefile, and this build
#   does not set CONFIG_CRYPTO, so the option is set here and never
#   reaches the compiler: the bignum comes from OpenSSL. Found by the
#   W15 check comparing .config against the real compile line, which is
#   the whole argument for reporting the flags the BINARY was built with.
# WHY CONFIG_ACS: carried from the original WPA3 build. The hwsim rig pins
#   the channel, so this changes nothing the sweep reaches; it is listed here
#   because it IS a difference from upstream, which is the only claim this
#   block makes about it.

# The other direction, and the one that is easy to miss: options upstream
# defconfig SETS that this build does not. Each is parse surface a run from
# this file cannot have covered, whatever its numbers say.

# WHY CONFIG_DPP CONFIG_DPP2: upstream defconfig ships these on and this
#   build does not. The cost is exact and is not hypothetical: the dpp-tcp
#   transmit target has no call site here, and dpp_check_attrs() -- one of
#   the four parsers W13 instrumented -- is not in this binary, so nothing
#   this build sends is ever read by it. Turn them on to sweep that surface;
#   leaving them off is a narrowing, not a default.
# WHY CONFIG_OWE: upstream defconfig sets it. Off here, so the OWE
#   Diffie-Hellman Parameter element is neither built nor parsed by this
#   binary. Same shape of loss as DPP, one element wide.
# WHY CONFIG_EAP_GTC: upstream sets it; this build carries MD5, TLS,
#   MSCHAPv2, PEAP and TTLS only. EAP method bodies ride inside EAPOL-EAP
#   frames, which no hook in this tree mutates -- the EAPOL-Key hooks sit
#   below EAP -- so this one costs the sweep nothing. It is listed because
#   the check does not let it go unlisted.
